\begin_layout Title
Set 7 - Regression Discontinuity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
A Formal Discussion of RDD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Regression Discontinuity Designs (RDD)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

RDD relies on treatment being a discontinuous function of some continuous
 observable, 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
 In a sharp RD, individuals on one side of a threshold, 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

, are treated while those on the other are controlled.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{i}=1(r>c)
\]

\end_inset

or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
T_{i}=1(r<c)
\]

\end_inset

We do no think that the average among controls provides a good counterfactual
 for the treated because of many underlying differences.
 Yet, as we approach the threshold, we think these differences shrink to
 zero.
 If this is true, then the difference in expected outcomes across the threshold
 will provide a valid estimate of the local average treatment effect (LATE),
 which is an average treatment effect for those individuals around the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Identifying Assumption:
\series default
\emph default
 As long as characteristics (observable and unobservable) are smooth and
 continuous across the cutoff, then we can get an unbiased estimate of the
 treatment by comparing expected outcomes across the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Academic probation
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $r:\mbox{GPA}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $T:1(\mbox{GPA}<1.5)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y:\mbox{Drop Out}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Unionization
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $r:\mbox{Vote shares}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $T:1(r>0.5)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $y:\mbox{Firm performance}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Class size (Angrist and Lavy)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $r:\mbox{School enrollment}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Postive Attributes of RD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to Lee and Lemieux (2010, JEL), RD is the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

second best
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 research design after experiments.
 For example, suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 has a random component, and the assignment rule is known to the researcher.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 were completely random, then this would be an experiment.
 We usually think that agents can influence and/or manipulate 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

, but it is not precise because there is error.
 As such, the probability of being just above versus being just below the
 threshold are equal.
 Thus, in the neighborhood around the threshold, variation in treatment
 is as good as randomly assigned.
 Plus, RD estimates can be interpreted as a weighted ATE where weights are
 the ex-ante probability that an individual's 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 will be in the neighborhood of 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Hence, RD is certainly the next best alternative when welfare considerations
 make randomized control trials infeasible.
 To illustrate, suppose policy makers wants to enact an intervention in
 which they believe some people will benefit more than others.
 Yet, those in charge do not feel comfortable excluding people from the
 intervention who they think will benefit the most.
 Consequently, they will probably not agree to participate in a randomized
 control trial.
 Instead, we can construct a RD design if we can get those in charge to
 order individuals in regards to 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

expected benefit,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and have them tell us where they draw the cutoff line.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Within-Study Comparison Studies
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Moreover, within-study comparison studies are favorable (see Thomas Cook's
 work).
 These studies originated with LaLonde (1986).
 In the setup, we have an experimentally assigned treatment group and control
 group.
 So, we can obtain an unbiased estimate of the ATE.
 Though, how well do we perform when we use a non-experimental comparison
 group with various methods?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
RD Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

With RD, we are trying to estimate the change in the conditional expectation
 as we crosse the treatment threshold, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\theta_{RD}={\displaystyle \lim_{r\rightarrow c}}E[y_{i}|r_{i}=r]-{\displaystyle \lim_{c\leftarrow r}}E[y_{i}|r_{i}=r]
\]

\end_inset

Standard non-parametric approaches do not perform well in this setup because
 we are interested in the conditional expectation function at a specific
 point and this point is at a boundary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Choices To Make
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Functional form: constant function; linear function; quadratic function;
 different slopes across and/or curvature across treatment threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bandwidth 
\begin_inset Formula $(h)$
\end_inset

: how far away from the threshold should we use data?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Global is equivalent to using all data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Local is equivalent to using data 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

near
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 the threshold, 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Weight: Rectangular; Triangular; Epanechnikov
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
The Choices Are Not Independent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
For smaller a 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, higher order polynomicals are less important.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Shrinking the bandwidth serves a similar purpose to giving more weight to
 observations near the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The choices involve tradeoff between bias and efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Shrinking the bandwidth: The chosen functional form is more likely to provide
 a good approximation of the truth.
 However, we compromise our ability to estimate this function with precision
 because we are using less data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Higher order polynomials: They are likely to fit the data better, but we
 may still lose precision because we are estimating more parameters.
 Moreover, we may make the fit worse at the threshold, which would increase
 bias.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
How wide should the bins for the data be? If the bins are too small, then
 the data becomes susceptible to noise.
 If the bins are too large, then there may be a problem with over-smoothing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Standard Practice
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

According to Imbens and Lemieux (2008, Journal of Econometrics), the standard
 practice for RD is to perform a local linear regression with rectangular
 kernel weights.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y|r]=\alpha+\theta1(r>c)+\beta r+\gamma r1(r>c)
\]

\end_inset

for 
\begin_inset Formula $r<c+h$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $r>c-h$
\end_inset

.
 With this setup, we are allowing for different slopes across the threshold.
 The control side is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha+\beta r
\]

\end_inset

while the treated side is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha+\theta+(\beta+\gamma)r
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Also, we often normalize the running variable, such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{r}=r-c$
\end_inset

.
 Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E[y|\tilde{r}]=\alpha+\theta1(\tilde{r}>0)+\beta\tilde{r}+\gamma\tilde{r}1(\tilde{r}>0)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\alpha}$
\end_inset

 gives the estimated mean at 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 (on the control side), which helps put the magnitude of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 in context.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Bandwidth Selection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Important to note, there is no agreed upon approach to choosing the bandwidth,
 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
 Here are some suggestions provided by Imbens and Kalyanamaran (2012, RESTUD):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Use intuition: We want to use a much data as possible (i.e., a large 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

) to gain efficiency, but it should not be so large that the model becomes
 misspecified.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
This provides a good reference, but it is not the truth.
 Remember, estimates can be sensitive to the starting values of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The optimal bandwidth theory is based on having continuous data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
When showing RD results, we need to provide bandwidth sensitivity analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Inference
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Lee and Card (2008) suggest that we cluster the standard errors on 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 when it is discrete.
 However, there is some controversy associated with this approach.
 Are we trying to estimate the true conditional expectation function, or
 the best linear approximation to it? If it is the latter, then clustering
 the standard errors on 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 when it is discrete should be fine.
 In fact, we are probably better off just clustering the standard errors
 on the running variable, which is more conservative.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Potential Threats to RD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Below are three common threats to RD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A misspecified functional form, particularly for large 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Precise manipulation of the running variable 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

.
 We are worried about this because it may lead to discontinuities in characteris
tics that are related to the outcome, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
E.g., student athletes and academic probation.
 There might be a discontinuity in Pr(athlete) across the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We prefer that the threshold is unknown when 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 is determined.
 Even if the threshold was unknown, we should still investigate empirically.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Non-Random Heaping (Barreca, Lindo, and Wadall)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Basically, any discontinuity in the conditional expectation function within
 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 will lead to bias.
 This can be especially problematic with non-random heaping.
 If variables related to outcomes predict heaping in 
\begin_inset Formula $r,$
\end_inset

 then estimates will usually be biased.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
To test for non-random heaping, look for heaps in disaggregated histograms.
 If there are heaps present, then they could be correlated with observables
 and/or unobservables.
 If we are concerned about this, then we can get unbiased estimates by separatel
y considering each 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

type.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Remember to consider all aspects relevant to experiments, such as attrition,
 non-compliance, etc.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Two Approaches
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

There are two approaches to deal with the potential threats present in a
 RDD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Perform a RD on observable covariates, and then show the observations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
According to McCrary (2008), we can also perform a RD on the distribution
 and then show the observations.
 This is a less straightforward than the other approach because it requires
 us to put the data into bins before estimation.
 Also, if we do this, then we should perform several estimations that each
 use different bin sizes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Fuzzy RD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A fuzzy RD occurs when the cutoff does not produce a change in the variable
 of interest that is equal to 1.
 If this occurs, then our RD in outcomes is an intent to treat (ITT) effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
The cutoff determines eligibility, but there are other criteria used in
 assignment.
 Or, the cutoff could trigger treatment, but compliance is not perfect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then, we can recover a local average treatment effect (LATE), just as with
 IV.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Recovering a LATE with IV
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\mbox{reduced form}}{\mbox{1st stage}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Recoving a LATE with fuzzy RD
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\mbox{discontinuity in outcomes}}{\mbox{discontinuity in treatment}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fuzzy RD approach addresses the possibility that compliance is correlated
 with potential outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose rich individuals receive treatment no matter what, but poorer individual
s only receive treatment when 
\begin_inset Formula $r>c$
\end_inset

.
 This is not a problem because we just consider rich individuals to be always
 takers, while poorer individuals are compliers.
 The fuzzy RD gives the ATE for poorer individuals with 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 close to 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
 In terms of estimation, the excluded instrument is the variable for being
 above the cutoff 
\begin_inset Formula $1(r>c)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Extensions of RD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We could vary the cutoffs across groups.
 To do this, we can either:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Estimate effects separately.
 If the effects are the same, then this supports generalizability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Normalize 
\begin_inset Formula $r$
\end_inset

 and pool the data, which is more efficient
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Suppose there are multiple cutoffs faced by the same individuals.
 If other cutoffs have effects, then including then in 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 will bias estimates (misspecifications)
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Implications for max allowable bandwidth
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Card et al.
 (2012) presented a regression kink design
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Instead of a discontinuity in 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, there is a change in the slope of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Then, we can consider if there is a corresponding slope change in outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
After tax income as a function of taxable income.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Multivariate RD design
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Multiple assignment variables with cutoffs used for treatment assignment.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
A pooling approach provides a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

frontier average treatment effect,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which is a weighted average of frontier specific effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
However, the average effect depends on a scale dependent weighting scheme,
 which is not good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Math and English tests for ES Exit (Papay 2011)
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Part*
A Brief Outline of RDD
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Basic Idea
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Treatment is determined at least in part by the value of a covariate, specifical
ly, whether it lies on one side of a fixed threshold or the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
\emph on
Identifying Assumption:
\series default
\emph default
 All other determinants of the outcome vary smoothly across the threshold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the identifying assumption to hold, individuals cannot be able to manipulate
 where they are relative to the cutoff (it helps if they don't even know
 where the cutoff is).
 Also, when nothing else besides the treatment changes at the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

RD can be invalid if individuals can precisely manipulate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

assignment variable.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 If individuals are unable to precisely manipulate the assignment variable,
 a consequence of this is that the variation in treatment near the threshold
 is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

randomized,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as if from a randomized experiment.
 Intuitively, when individuals have imprecise control over the assignment
 variable, even if some are especially likely to have values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 near the cutoff, every individual will have approximately the same probability
 of having 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 just above or just below the cutoff.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Sharp RD: obtain average treatment effect for individuals near the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Fuzzy RD: Obtain a local average treatment effect, interpreted as the average
 effect on compliers near the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Fuzzy RD is valid under the assumption that there are no defiers, which
 is what we always assume.
 Then, the LATE captures the treatment effect for compliers.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

RDD has significant upsides.
 Under the identifying assumption, RD offers a clean and transparent way
 of obtaining a causal effect.
 Plus, the identifying assumption is somewhat testable.
 RD can be analyzed, and tested, like randomized experiments.
 However, RDD still has some downsides as well.
 Obtaining precise estimates can be difficult, and estimates are only relevant
 for the population near the cutoff.
 Additionally, in a fuzzy RD, the estimates are only relevant for compliers
 near the cutoff.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Difference Between RD and IV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For IV, we must assume the instrument is exogenously generated as if by
 a coin flip.
 Such an assumption is often difficult to justify.
 By contrast, the variation that the RD design isolates is randomized as
 a consequence of the assumption that individuals have imprecise control
 over the assignment variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
RD can be invalid if individuals can precisely manipulate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

assignment variable.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
If individuals are unable to precisely manipulate the assignment variable,
 a consequene of this is that the variation in treatment near the threshold
 is randomized as though from a randomized 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Note*
Including fixed effects is unnecessary for identification in a RDD.
 This sharply contrasts with a more traditional panel data setting.
 The only rationale to include fixed effects is to reduce sample variance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Test RDD Identifying Assumption
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Look for discontinuities in pre-determined or exogenous characteristics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Show RD estimates with and without controls
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Good outcome: Estimate is the same.
 Treatment is orthogonal to controls, at least once you control for the
 running variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Bad outcome: Estimate changes in a significant way.
 There is manipulation around the cutoff, as controls are systematically
 correlated with being above the cutoff, conditional on the running variable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Concern: If treatment and control vary on things we can observe, what about
 things we can't observe?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Look for 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

lumpiness
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in the density function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Look for discontinuities in the outcome at fake cutoffs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
RDD Robustness Tests:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the right functional form?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Look at the data and see how well you are fitting it.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Show results from multiple functional forms
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What is the appropriate bandwidth?
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Tradeoff: Bigger bandwidths lead to more precise estimates that are less
 susceptible to noise.
 Though, with bigger bandwidths, observations far from the cutoff can influence
 the RD estimate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Solution: Show data with large bandwidth and present estimates with narrower
 bandwidths.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What the running variable is discrete, you need to cluster the standard
 errors at the level of the clusters in the running variable.
\end_layout

